<p>
  One of the most practical questions Plano homeowners ask is whether their kitchen or bathroom remodel needs a permit — and whether their HOA has a say. The short answer: most full remodels that touch structure or plumbing and electrical work will need permits, and many North Dallas neighborhoods have HOA rules you should check before demo day.
</p>

<h2>When a permit is typically required</h2>
<p>
  In Plano, permits are generally needed when a remodel changes the structure of the home or alters its plumbing, electrical, or mechanical systems. A few examples that usually trigger permit requirements:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Removing or altering walls, including opening up a kitchen into a great room.</li>
  <li>Relocating plumbing — moving a sink, shower, toilet, or adding an island with a sink or dishwasher.</li>
  <li>Adding or relocating electrical circuits, outlets, or fixtures.</li>
  <li>Ventilation changes, such as adding or relocating bathroom exhaust fans.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Cosmetic refreshes that keep fixtures in place — new cabinets in the same footprint, painting, and new flooring — are more likely to fall outside permit requirements, but the safe move is to confirm with the City of Plano permit office for your specific project.
</p>

<h2>Why permits matter</h2>
<p>
  A permit means the work is inspected against code, which protects your family and your home's value. If you ever sell, a properly permitted remodel is a cleaner story for buyers and appraisers. Unpermitted work can surface during a home inspection or a future remodel and cost far more to resolve than the permit did.
</p>

<h2>HOA approvals in Plano neighborhoods</h2>
<p>
  Depending on where you live, your homeowners association may have its own approval process. Homes in neighborhoods like <strong>Whiffletree, Willow Bend, Lakeside on Preston, Russell Creek, Gleneagles, and Los Rios</strong> often sit in HOA-managed communities, and townhomes and condos (for example around Austin Ranch) may require board approval for interior work that affects common elements or the building envelope.
</p>
<p>
  HOA requirements vary widely, so check your community's guidelines before starting. Your contractor can help you understand what documents the HOA typically wants, but the application itself is usually your responsibility.
</p>

